# Ontology-first Ash with ggen

AshR2ML's ontology-first workflow treats generated Ash resources as projections of an admitted semantic model.

The source of truth is not the generated Elixir file.

```text
RDF/OWL vocabulary
      ↓
application profile
      ↓
SHACL operational shapes
      ↓
ggen
      ↓
generated Ash resources
      ↓
AshR2ML.Mapping
      ↓
R2RML
```

## Why SHACL sits between OWL and Ash

OWL is open-world and does not necessarily specify enough operational information to choose a relational representation.

Ash and relational schemas need decisions such as:

```text
is the value required?
is it single-valued?
what datatype is stored?
what identifies a subject?
is this an entity or an action?
which relation owns the foreign key?
```

The application profile and SHACL shapes provide that closure.

AshR2ML does not claim arbitrary OWL-to-SQL compilation.

## Start with a domain vocabulary

A public or application ontology may define:

```turtle
@prefix ex: <https://example.org/ontology/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

ex:Person a owl:Class .
ex:Organization a owl:Class .

ex:name a owl:DatatypeProperty .
ex:memberOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
  rdfs:domain ex:Person ;
  rdfs:range ex:Organization .
```

These facts describe meaning, but they do not determine a complete Ash/PostgreSQL schema.

## Add an application profile and operational shape

```turtle
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix r2ml: <https://ash-r2ml.dev/ns#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

ex:OrganizationShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass ex:Organization ;
    r2ml:ashModule "MyApp.Organization" ;
    r2ml:table "organizations" ;
    r2ml:subjectTemplate "https://example.org/organizations/{id}" ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:name ;
        sh:datatype xsd:string ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1
    ] .

ex:PersonShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass ex:Person ;
    r2ml:ashModule "MyApp.Person" ;
    r2ml:table "people" ;
    r2ml:subjectTemplate "https://example.org/people/{id}" ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:name ;
        sh:datatype xsd:string ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path ex:memberOf ;
        sh:class ex:Organization ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1
    ] .
```

The operational shape now gives the compiler enough information to manufacture a required scalar and a required to-one relationship.

## Compile with the AshR2ML ggen pack

The project imports the AshR2ML pack and runs the standard ggen synchronization path.

Conceptually:

```bash
ggen sync
```

The pack performs:

```text
parse RDF
  ↓
load application profile
  ↓
run semantic admission gates
  ↓
project normalized resource/attribute/relationship facts
  ↓
render Ash resources
  ↓
render AshR2ML semantic annotations
  ↓
write deterministic projections
```

The exact project configuration is ordinary ggen configuration; there is no second bespoke generator runtime inside AshR2ML.

## Generated resource shape

The person resource manufactured from the example profile is equivalent to:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Person do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: MyApp.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshR2ML.Resource]

  postgres do
    table "people"
    repo MyApp.Repo
  end

  r2rml do
    class "https://example.org/ontology/Person"

    subject do
      template "https://example.org/people/{id}"
      term_type :iri
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :name, :string do
      allow_nil? false

      rdf do
        predicate "https://example.org/ontology/name"
      end
    end
  end

  relationships do
    belongs_to :organization, MyApp.Organization do
      allow_nil? false

      rdf do
        predicate "https://example.org/ontology/memberOf"
      end
    end
  end
end
```

Do not hand-edit this generated module. Change the profile or compiler source and regenerate.

## Admission before rendering

The pack fails before writing generated files when the semantic model is incomplete or ambiguous.

Examples of refused inputs:

```text
NodeShape has no target class
mapped resource has no subject identity
property shape has no datatype or target class
relationship target has no mapped resource
subject template references an unknown field
object property admits several incompatible storage strategies
an asserted close match is treated as equivalence without proof
many-valued relation is forced into one scalar column
```

The generator should never repair these by convention.

## Nouns, relationships, and actions

Ontology-first generation is structural, not lexical.

A class or node shape representing a durable subject can become an Ash resource.

An object property becomes a relationship when the profile admits that interpretation.

A verb-like operation should not become a table merely because an ontology individual or capability name contains a noun phrase.

Application behavior belongs in Ash actions; persistent entities belong in resources.

## Cardinality correspondence

Typical shape-to-Ash correspondence:

| SHACL | Ash candidate | Relational consequence |
|---|---|---|
| `sh:minCount 1` scalar | `allow_nil? false` | `NOT NULL` where applicable |
| `sh:maxCount 1` scalar | one attribute | one column/value |
| `sh:class` + `sh:maxCount 1` | `belongs_to` / `has_one` | FK candidate |
| many-valued object property | `has_many` / `many_to_many` | FK/join candidate |
| relation with attributes | association resource | relationship table/resource |

These are candidates until the application profile determines direction, ownership, and identity.

## Datatype correspondence

The pack maps supported RDF datatypes to Ash types through the same datatype registry used by AshR2ML runtime mapping.

For example:

```text
xsd:string   → :string
xsd:boolean  → :boolean
xsd:integer  → :integer
xsd:decimal  → :decimal
xsd:date     → :date
xsd:dateTime → UTC datetime type
```

An unknown datatype is not converted to a string automatically.

## Semantic identity

The profile must declare how relational records become RDF subjects.

A subject template may use one or several admitted identity fields:

```turtle
r2ml:subjectTemplate "https://example.org/accounts/{tenant_id}/{account_number}" .
```

The generated Ash resource should carry compatible identity constraints when the profile declares uniqueness.

RDF identity remains stable even if the internal database uses a surrogate UUID.

## Public ontologies remain public

Do not add Ash-specific facts directly to imported public ontology files.

Use a profile layer:

```text
public ontology
  ex:Person
      │
      ▼
application profile
  maps ex:Person → MyApp.Person
      │
      ▼
SHACL operational shape
  cardinality/type/identity/storage facts
```

This allows the same public ontology to support several lawful application projections without contaminating its vocabulary with implementation details.

## Conservative semantic alignment

The compiler preserves the distinction between:

```text
owl:equivalentClass
rdfs:subClassOf
skos:exactMatch
skos:closeMatch
application-specific mapping
```

It never upgrades a weaker assertion to `owl:equivalentClass` merely to simplify generation.

## Determinism

Run generation twice against the same admitted graph.

The generated Ash resources and mapping artifacts must be byte-stable except for explicitly declared nondeterministic receipt fields.

A normal verification pattern is:

```bash
ggen sync
git diff --exit-code -- generated/
```

or the consuming repository's equivalent generated-surface verifier.

## End-to-end semantic proof

The ontology-first crown is not generation alone.

The complete proof is:

```text
ontology + SHACL
      ↓
ggen
      ↓
Ash resources
      ↓
AshPostgres / relational DB
      ↓
AshR2ML R2RML
      ↓
OBDA engine
      ↓
SPARQL
```

For the admitted fixture graph, the SPARQL-visible subjects, classes, scalar properties, and object-property relationships must correspond to the same records observed through Ash.

That closes the semantic round trip without requiring a graph database.
